   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of Member States to formulate and implement national employment policies in response to the COVID-19 crisis</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">IIn September 2021, the Government of Botswana has adopted the country&#x2019;s first national employment policy (NEP). In December 2023, the NEP Task Team, which includes representatives from workers&#x2019; organizations, (Botswana Federation of Trade Unions and Botswana Federation of Public, Private and Parastatal Sector Unions) as well as the employers&#x2019; organizations (Business Botswana) developed and adopted  the NEP implementation plan to operationalise the policy. The implementation period is April 2024 to March 2029. 
The implementation plan puts employment at the centre of development programmes by promoting pro-employment development and maximising local beneficiation. The implementation plan also seeks to harness green job opportunities resulting from the country&#x2019;s efforts towards sustainable development and addressing climate change.  
In this biennium, just transition was put on the map and became part of the national conversation in Botswana. In that regard, Botswana recognises that inclusive growth is only possible if the transition towards a low-carbon and sustainable economy delivers decent work and social justice and serves as a vehicle for poverty reduction for women and men in all their diversity, with policies reflecting concerns for impacts in terms of decent work and gender equality dimensions. To this end, a draft Tripartite Action Plan for a Just Transition in Botswana was developed in 2023, based on submissions by workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations and Government stakeholders. 
The implementation plan for the NEP is also a key avenue for the promotion of a just transition in Botswana though a concrete actions in selected economic sectors, i.e., agriculture, mining and tourism. The ILO provided support towards the development of the NEP Implementation Plan i.e., technical support (direct drafting support and technical advice) which led to the finalisation of the Plan. This also included a dedicated capacity development programme focused on the implementation of national employment policies, supported by the ILO and based on ILO&#x2019;s guidelines. In support of the Government&#x2019;s efforts towards sustainable development, the Government of Botswana and Social Partners, the ILO launched the project &#x201c;Addressing Climate Change and Promoting a Just Transition to a Greener World of Work in Botswana&#x201d; to address the impacts of climate change and unsustainable practices on the world of work.
The project, amongst other initiatives, aimed to support the Government of Botswana and Social Partners in the preparation of a Tripartite Action Plan on Just Transition, as a means to further inform national development strategies and the design and implementation of just transition strategies and policies. The constituents &#x2013; the Workers&#x2019; organisations BFTU and BOFEPUSU; the Employers and Business Membership Organisation Business Botswana and the Women in Business Association (WIBA), as well as the Government stakeholders met separately to put together their submissions to inform the Tripartite Action Plan.
Subsequently, a tripartite national workshop to develop a Tripartite Action Plan on Just Transition in Botswana took place on 6-8 December 2023 in Gaborone with support from GIZ-funded ILO project on Delivering a Gender-Transformative Just Transition. The workshop offered a platform to engage in social dialogue on just transition and its gender dimensions in Botswana. Based on this work, a first draft of the Tripartite Action Plan on Just Transition was developed with ILO technical and financial support.</narrative>
   </description>
